    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.8",
    "topic_title": "Representations of Solutions",
    "stem": "Two particulate models represent aqueous NaCl samples. Model 1 represents a volume V and shows four Na⁺ symbols and four Cl⁻ symbols. Model 2 represents a volume 3V and shows six Na⁺ symbols and six Cl⁻ symbols. Each ion symbol represents the same number of ions in both models; water molecules are omitted. What is the ratio [Na⁺]₁/[Na⁺]₂?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "2/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Particle-count-only trap: Dividing four by six compares ion counts, not concentrations. The models represent different volumes, so their counts must first be normalized by volume. Fix: Divide each Na⁺ count by its model’s volume before comparing."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "2",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Na⁺ concentration is proportional to the represented Na⁺ count divided by the represented volume. Therefore, [Na⁺]₁/[Na⁺]₂ = (4/V)/(6/(3V)) = 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "1/2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-ratio trap: This value is [Na⁺]₂/[Na⁺]₁, the reciprocal of the requested ratio. Model 1 has twice the Na⁺ concentration of model 2. Fix: Put model 1’s concentration in the numerator and model 2’s in the denominator."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Volume-only trap: An inverse-volume comparison assumes equal numbers of Na⁺ ions. Model 2 represents more Na⁺ ions, so volume alone cannot determine the concentration ratio. Fix: Include both the Na⁺ count and represented volume for each model."
     }
    ]
   },
