   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.8",
    "topic_title": "Representations of Solutions",
    "stem": "Two particulate diagrams of aqueous solutions are described in words; water molecules are omitted and every drawn ion represents the same number of moles. Diagram P represents a 0.50 L sample of KCl(aq) and shows 4 K⁺ ions and 4 Cl⁻ ions. Diagram Q represents a 2.0 L sample of MgCl₂(aq) and shows 6 Mg²⁺ ions and 12 Cl⁻ ions. Which statement correctly compares the chloride ion concentration, [Cl⁻], in the two solutions?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "[Cl⁻] is greater in the MgCl₂ solution because its diagram shows three times as many Cl⁻ ions.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This compares raw particle counts as if both diagrams showed equal volumes. Diagram Q represents four times the volume of P, so its 12 Cl⁻ ions are spread out to 6 per liter versus 8 per liter for KCl. Fix: Divide each particle count by its diagram's stated volume before comparing concentrations."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "[Cl⁻] in the MgCl₂ solution is twice [Cl⁻] in the KCl solution because each MgCl₂ releases two Cl⁻ ions.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes the two solutions have equal solute concentrations and applies only the 2:1 dissociation ratio. The diagrams give actual ion counts and volumes, which show MgCl₂ is less concentrated, so the Cl⁻ ratio is not 2. Fix: Compute [Cl⁻] from the drawn counts and volumes instead of from formula subscripts alone."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "[Cl⁻] in the KCl solution is 8/3 times [Cl⁻] in the MgCl₂ solution.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This compares solute formula-unit concentrations (8 KCl per liter versus 3 MgCl₂ per liter) rather than chloride concentrations. Each MgCl₂ contributes two Cl⁻, so MgCl₂ gives 6 Cl⁻ per liter, not 3. Fix: Ask which particle the question names, then count only that ion per liter."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "[Cl⁻] in the KCl solution is 4/3 times [Cl⁻] in the MgCl₂ solution.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Concentration is particles per volume, so each count must be divided by the volume its diagram represents. KCl: 4 Cl⁻ ÷ 0.50 L = 8 per liter; MgCl₂: 12 Cl⁻ ÷ 2.0 L = 6 per liter. The ratio 8 to 6 equals 4/3."
     }
    ]
   },
